WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
 I think you can see that Tim Berners-Lee is a real bright guy with a real strong vision

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:11.060
 of the future and a real deep commitment to the web and the Internet being an agent of

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:14.140
 change in the world.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:20.440
 And I think that it's going to continue to be that unless proprietary companies can get

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:21.440
 an upper hand.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.940
 And so it's really incumbent on all of us to protect the freedom of the Internet.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:32.020
 And believe me, there are organizations that think they should run the Internet and the

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:33.020
 web.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:34.020
 They just are.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.420
 They never have stopped.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:40.960
 So we're at the point where the web is all here.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.580
 It's quite amazing.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:46.660
 And before we move on, I want to characterize just a little bit about the people you've

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:54.740
 met so far and talk about some of the common things that they have between them.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:01:01.100
 And in a way, I've got the book The Wisdom of Crowds here, one of my favorite books.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:04.260
 Each one of these people, when you listen to them talk, they would quite often talk

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.540
 about all the other people doing all the other work.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:10.460
 And they rarely consider themselves in any way heroes.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:13.460
 They rarely consider themselves as the great innovators.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:17.340
 They always see themselves as part of a collective curiosity.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.340
 And there were many things that I haven't told you about that were curious explorations

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:25.460
 that helped inform it.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:29.060
 And this was really research until sort of the late '90s.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:32.020
 It was just a question of how to do it.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:38.700
 And at some point after the late '90s, it switched to what to do with it.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:44.300
 And at that point, we have a whole different group of people that start really entering

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.840
 the world.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:52.420
 And one that's one of the earlier ones and has benefited greatly from it is Jeff Bezos.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.380
 He's the founder of Amazon.com.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.380
 And if you listen to Jeff, you will hear that he talks very differently.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:02.220
 He comes at it from a business perspective.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.300
 He's not necessarily trying to make the world a worse place.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.540
 He's trying to make the world a better place.

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:11.060
 The fact that you can get all these books like The Next Morning, that's kind of cool.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.440
 So what Amazon does is really wonderful.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.300
 It's more efficient for society, it's economically beneficial.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.700
 But he has a profit in mind.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:23.580
 He is saying this web exists, browsers exist, protocols exist.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.580
 How can I take best advantage of it?

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:31.580
 Richard Wiggins caught up with Jeff in 1997.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:38.180
 So just look at how smart this guy was in 1997.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:42.060
 Everyone else figured this out much later, but Jeff Bezos is a really, really bright

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.060
 person.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.060
 Here's Jeff Bezos.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.060
 Hi there.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:46.060
 Who are you?

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:47.060
 I'm Jeff Bezos.

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:50.060
 What is your claim to fame?

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:52.060
 I'm the founder of Amazon.com.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:55.060
 Where did you get an idea for Amazon.com?

00:02:55.060 --> 00:03:00.060
 Well, three years ago I was in New York City working for a quantitative hedge fund when

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:05.060
 it came across the startling statistic that web usage was growing at 2300% a year.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:10.060
 So I decided I would try and find a business plan that made sense in the context of that

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:11.060
 growth.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:15.060
 So I picked books as the first best product to sell online for making a list of like 20

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.780
 different products that you might be able to sell.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:22.940
 Books were great as the first best because books are incredibly unusual in one respect.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:27.260
 That is that there are more items in the book category than there are items in any other

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.100
 category by far.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.100
 Music is number two.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:32.900
 There are about 200,000 active music CDs at any given time.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:37.700
 But in the book space, there are more than three million different books worldwide active

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.500
 and in print at any given time across all languages.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.380
 More than one and a half million in English alone.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:49.100
 So when you have that many items you can literally build a store online that couldn't exist any

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:50.100
 other way.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.740
 And that's important right now because the web is still an infant technology.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:57.860
 Basically right now if you can do things using a more traditional method, you probably should

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:00.940
 do them using the more traditional method.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:03.120
 What kind of inventory do you keep?

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.620
 We inventory the best selling books at any given time.

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:11.100
 We're inventorying in our own warehouse only a couple of thousand titles.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:15.740
 And then we have, we do almost in time inventory for another 400,000 titles or so.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:20.620
 We get those from a network of electronic, we order electronically from a network of

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.480
 wholesalers and distributors.

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:23.980
 We order those today.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:26.700
 They're on our loading dock the next morning.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:33.020
 Then for another 1.1 million titles we get those directly from 20,000 different publishers

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:35.620
 and those can take a couple of weeks to get.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:40.140
 And then there are a million out of print books in our catalog.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:43.100
 We have a catalog of two and a half million books altogether.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:46.420
 Those million out of print books, some of them we can get and some of them we can't.

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:50.300
 But we find them if we can and then we ship them to our customers.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:53.300
 We do kind of a search on those.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.380
 What's almost in time inventory?

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:59.820
 Almost in time inventory is the phrase we use to describe a whole selection of books

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:00.820
 that we offer.

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:07.240
 Particularly the things that are below the 2,000th best selling book up to the 400,000th

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.500
 best selling book.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:12.340
 Those are titles that we can get from a network of more than a dozen different wholesalers.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:16.980
 So if a customer orders a book from us today, we order that book from our wholesalers today

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:21.400
 and that book shows up on our loading dock the next morning.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.580
 And then we can ship it to the customer.

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:27.180
 They say one of the toughest things to do on the internet is to capture mind share.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:28.180
 What was your secret?

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:29.180
 How did you do that?

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:34.020
 Well yeah, even more generally, I agree with you that capturing mind share on the internet

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:35.260
 is extremely difficult.

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:39.700
 Even more generally, it's the late 20th century, not just the internet.

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:45.420
 Capturing attention, attention is the scarce commodity of the late 20th century and one

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.940
 of the ways that you can do that and it's the way that we did it was by doing something

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:54.800
 new and innovative for the first time that actually has real value for the customer.

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:59.260
 It's a hard thing to do, but if you do do that, then newspapers will write about you,

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:00.260
 what you're doing.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:05.420
 Customers will tell other customers and you'll get a huge word of mouth fan out and that

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:08.580
 can really drive and accelerate businesses and that's what happened with us.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:13.780
 In the first year of opening amazon.com to the public, we didn't do any paid advertising

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:18.700
 and all of our growth was fueled by word of mouth and media exposure.

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:21.900
 I saw little ads at the bottom of the column of the New York Times.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.580
 That was our very first advertising.

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:29.540
 We don't do that anymore, but at the very beginning, we did little tiny ads at the bottom

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:31.980
 of the front page of the New York Times.

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:32.980
 I thought that was very clever.

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:36.900
 It's sort of using a URL as a macro because I read that it expands.

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:38.380
 We're a bookstore.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:39.380
 Click here.

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:40.380
 Right.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:41.380
 That's a great way to think of it.

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:43.420
 And it worked very well apparently.

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:47.580
 I don't know, you know, the problem with that kind of advertising is it's extremely difficult

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:48.580
 to track.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:52.700
 Put a different URL for every...

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:55.660
 That's the problem is that you want people to start to learn your URL so you don't want

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:57.580
 to actually use a different one.

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:00.260
 And it's very easy.

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:07.380
 One of the great things about online ads, we do advertising today in maybe 40 different

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:08.380
 websites.

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:09.380
 We do banner ads.

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:14.380
 And that advertising is very easy to track in terms of knowing how effective it is.

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:19.500
 So we know for each piece of creative in each venue, not only how many click throughs we

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:25.420
 get but how many sell throughs we get, how many dollars of revenue it generates per ad

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:28.140
 dollar spent on that creative in that venue.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:34.060
 And that is sort of a marketer's nirvana in a certain sense.

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.460
 Well it's an exciting place to be on the web right now.

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:37.700
 Oh, it absolutely is.

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:39.740
 I mean it's just incredible.

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:42.780
 What's really incredible about this is that this is day one.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:43.860
 This is the very beginning.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:47.060
 This is the Kitty Hawk stage of electronic commerce.

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:50.580
 We're moving forward in so many different areas and lots of different companies are

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:51.580
 as well.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:55.660
 And the late 20th century is just a great time to be alive.

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:59.740
 We're going to find out that I think a millennia from now people are going to look back and

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:06.460
 say wow, the late 20th century was really a great time to be alive on this planet.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:11.500
 So if you're a stockholder of Amazon, I think you're in good shape because he's a bright

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:17.140
 fellow and he's always looking for how to really do the right thing and give us products

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:18.900
 that we really like.

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:22.940
 Now in this we sort of evolve the modern internet.

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:28.220
 And much like Jeff, Jeff said that investment was growing at 3,000% a year or something

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:30.160
 like that.

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:35.700
 There was this mania in '96, '97, '98 where people just bought and bought and bought and

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:41.280
 the notion that communications was going to be so valuable led to in a sense an overbuilding

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:43.100
 of the fiber optic plan.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:48.940
 We have a massive amount of fiber optic in the ground right now and our ability to send

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:53.340
 more data through the existing fibers by just upgrading the equipment at the two ends, that's

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:55.540
 growing very rapidly as well.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:09:01.020
 And so we're in this really weird world now where a fiber optic between Ann Arbor and

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:11.300
 Chicago, 180 miles, is not that much more expensive than a piece of copper wire that goes 30 miles

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:13.140
 in 1960.

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:18.820
 And so distance is less and less important in the internet.

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:22.540
 It's why long distance phone calls don't cost any money anymore because long distance and

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:26.180
 local are not that different because there's so much fiber in the ground.

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:33.420
 But just one thing I want you to think about and wonder about is will we run out?

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:38.300
 We're living off of the overbuilding that happened in the late '90s when they put so

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:42.020
 much in and they've been able to upgrade so effectively.

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:46.460
 But we might run out and where will the money come from to dig the next set of trenches

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:50.780
 to put the next round of fiber in, we'll have to see.

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:57.380
 So if we take a quick look at-- this is a graph of the growth of the servers on the

00:09:57.380 --> 00:09:59.100
 World Wide Web.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:06.920
 And if you take a look, we start in 1990 with one web server, December 1990, with Tim Berners-Lee

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:08.800
 having one web server.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:10.220
 And then it sort of doesn't grow.

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:14.580
 And then you sort of see kind of like this takeoff in the last two years, last late two

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:23.500
 years of the 1990s, where you go up to about 20 to 30 million from what, I don't know,

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:28.980
 1996 is less than a quarter of a million dollars and less than a quarter of a million hosts.

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:34.820
 You see kind of the crash, the boom, and the crash where it wasn't growing.

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:39.720
 And then you sort of see another new graph, which is really-- this is when everybody had

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:40.900
 to be on the internet.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:43.460
 Every little store started being on the internet.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:45.460
 Every one had to have a web server.

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:47.540
 This was kind of the big ones coming on.

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:52.820
 And this was sort of like the flame out of the exuberant growth of the late '90s.

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:58.540
 And this exuberant growth gets so exuberant that it really changes the stock market in

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:03.500
 the United States and the rest of the world to the point where, you know, from the early

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:08.060
 '90s to 2000 was the longest period of expansion.

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:10.420
 And there's a lot of factors for this.

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:15.700
 But one of the factors is, as we began to use communication and computation to make

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:21.540
 commerce more efficient, that triggered a great deal of growth.

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:24.540
 It also triggered over-speculation, right?

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:32.380
 And so the kind of-- this part here, the crash, the dot com crash, was a bit of the over-speculation

00:11:32.380 --> 00:11:34.020
 working its way out.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:37.420
 Probably if, you know, if they hadn't over-speculated, it would go like this instead.

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:38.540
 But it can't be like that.

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:42.760
 You go crazy, crazy, crazy, crazy, crazy, oops, oops, oops, oops, oops.

00:11:42.760 --> 00:11:46.020
 And then we sort of had a crappy economy for a while after that.

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:51.540
 But again, the web, the internet, and all the technology that I just talked about really

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:57.380
 put a stamp on the entire world's economy in the 1990s.

00:11:57.380 --> 00:12:02.760
 So I could go on and on and on and on about this stuff.

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:05.380
 Open source is a big part of this.

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:10.220
 I will put links up for these videos that I've got.

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:13.880
 These are three open source luminaries that I've interviewed.

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:21.460
 Richard Stallman is the Free Software Foundation, the GNU, which is-- much of Unix's utilities

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:22.460
 are written this way.

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:24.780
 The compilers are written this way.

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:29.700
 He is a tremendous advocate for open source software.

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:33.220
 Also we meet Brian Bellendorf from the Apache Foundation.

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:39.740
 The Apache Foundation was formed based on the web server that was created at NCSA, became

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:42.460
 the first web server that was the Apache web server.

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:46.660
 So that lineage goes all the way back to Joseph Harden and the team at NCSA.

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:51.980
 And then Rasmus Lierdorf is the inventor of PHP and one of the many very popular web programming

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:54.100
 languages for building websites.

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:57.220
 And so I'll put the links up for these, and you can take a look at them.

